{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed262781723/2/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b0280bc\" lines=\"54-69\">\n<description>\nRecolor the shortest path between two terminal squares in a square-and-track graph.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Two non-bg colors form solid NxN squares (N>=2): one color marks exactly 2 terminals, the other marks stations.\n- A third non-bg color forms 1-cell-wide track segments connecting pairs of squares (graph edges).\n\nRule:\n- Find the shortest path (fewest edges) between the two terminal squares.\n- Recolor every station square on that path to green.\n- Recolor every track segment along that path to grey.\n- Everything else unchanged.\n\nKey insight: The grid encodes a graph where squares are nodes and tracks are edges; the task is graph shortest-path, not spatial proximity.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"6ffbe589\" lines=\"83-98\">\n<description>\nCrop a board cluster and rotate its color layers based on outside cue sprites.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- A main multi-color board cluster (largest foreground region).\n- Small monochrome cue sprites outside the board for the colors that rotate.\n\nRule:\n- Tight-crop around the board cluster.\n- For each color layer inside the crop, find its same-color cue outside.\n- Rotate that layer clockwise by (cue pixel count mod 4) × 90°.\n- No outside cue → no rotation.\n\nKey insight: Cue sprite size mod 4 encodes rotation amount for its matching color layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"83b6b474\" lines=\"95-111\">\n<description>\nTranslate the colored objects into the most balanced complete rectangular frame.\n\nInput structure\nA grid with one dominant background color and several monochromatic\n4-connected foreground objects that are straight bars or corner-like line\nsprites.\n\nRule\nPreserve every foreground object exactly, allowing translation only, and\nplace the objects so they cover every pixel of a one-cell-thick rectangular\nframe exactly once; the frame interior is the background color.\n\nKey insight\nThe output shape is not copied from the input: the foreground pixel count\ndetermines possible frame sizes, and the intended output is the valid frame\nassembly with the least difference between height and width.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e87109e9\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nEmit four teal beams from a square, turning at colored obstacles per a legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- Top band: grey (5) legend with colored vertical bars in cells; bar position (left or right) encodes turn direction.\n- Below: main grid with bg, a solid teal (8) square, and colored obstacle blocks.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the legend from the output.\n- From the teal square, shoot a beam in each cardinal direction (as a sliding copy of the square).\n- Each beam paints teal over bg cells as it moves.\n- On hitting an obstacle of color C, consult the legend: bar on left → turn counterclockwise, bar on right → turn clockwise. Continue in the new direction.\n- Beams stop at grid edges.\n\nKey insight: The legend's bar position (left vs right within its cell) is the encoding for turn direction at each obstacle color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e8686506\" lines=\"172-185\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered colored fragments into a left-right symmetric rectangle, jigsaw-style.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; remaining colors form small sprite fragments.\n- Optional roles: 4 singleton pixels of one color mark corners; horizontal bars mark top/bottom edges; paired vertical lines mark left/right edges.\n\nRule:\n- Collect all non-bg connected components as jigsaw pieces.\n- Determine output rectangle dimensions from total piece area.\n- Pack pieces into the rectangle so it is exactly mirror-symmetric (left-right) and same-color pieces never 4-touch.\n\nKey insight: Left-right symmetry plus the no-same-color-touching constraint make the assembly unique; corner/edge markers anchor it when present.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"2ccd9fef\" lines=\"173-187\">\n<description>\nPredict the next panel in a framed sequence by continuing each scheduled colored interval.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is made from three or more equal panels in one row or one column.\n- The final input panel is the static target template; every earlier panel differs from it only by dynamic colors absent from the template.\n- Each dynamic color appears in a regular temporal schedule across the earlier panels.\n\nRule\n- Keep the final template panel as the output canvas.\n- For every dynamic color whose schedule reaches the next panel, compare its last two appearances.\n- Extrapolate each one-dimensional interval along the color's changing row or column axis and paint that predicted mask.\n\nKey insight\n- The task is not asking for the last visible panel, but for the next frame after the blank/template slot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"b0039139\" lines=\"29-44\">\n<description>\nRecolor a cropped template and repeat it according to a component-count stencil.\n\nInput structure:\n- Solid blue (1) separator rows or columns split the grid into four sections.\n- Section 1 is a black-background shape template; section 2 is a component-count stencil.\n- Sections 3 and 4 are solid color swatches for output foreground and background.\n\nRule:\n- Crop section 1 to the bbox of its non-black pixels and recolor it with the two swatches.\n- Count 4-connected non-black components in section 2.\n- Repeat the recolored crop that many times, inserting one background-colored row/column between copies.\n\nKey insight: The second section encodes only the repeat count; the output orientation\nis inherited from whether the input separators are columns or rows.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6350f1f4\" lines=\"62-81\">\n<description>\nDecode a block-grid image by swapping each sprite-coded cell with a solid cell and each blank cell with the sprite.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid is partitioned by black separator rows and columns into equal-size\nsquare blocks. Each block is either a copy of one two-color sprite or a\nuniform blank block of the sprite's minority color; grey pixels are erased\ncells that act as wildcards.\n\nRule\nInfer the repeated sprite from fully visible mixed-color blocks. Replace a\nblock matching that sprite, ignoring grey erasures, with a solid block of\nthe sprite's majority color. Replace a uniform blank block with the sprite.\nSeparator rows and columns, including grey erasures on them, become black.\n\nKey insight\nThe small sprite is a block-level code for one macro-cell state, and the\nuniform minority-color block is the other state; the output renders the\nopposite code in each macro-cell while cleaning erased pixels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"f560132c\" lines=\"259-273\">\n<description>\nAssemble scattered jigsaw pieces into a rectangle, recoloring each by a legend sprite's color map.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several monochrome non-bg sprites (the \"pieces\"), scattered across the grid.\n- One sprite is the legend: it contains a multi-color k×k patch (typically 2×2) encoding output colors.\n\nRule:\n- Tile all pieces (rotations/reflections allowed) into a tight rectangle with no gaps.\n- The legend's k×k color patch defines a spatial color map over the rectangle.\n- Each piece is recolored to the legend-map color corresponding to its position in the assembled rectangle.\n\nKey insight: The legend piece doubles as both a jigsaw piece and a color key — its internal k×k palette assigns colors by spatial quadrant.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"22425bda\" lines=\"58-76\">\n<description>\nList the colors of overlaid straight lines in their only possible bottom-to-top crossing order.\n\nInput structure\nThe background is the dominant color. Every foreground color is the visible part\nof one full one-cell-thick horizontal, vertical, or 45-degree diagonal line.\nHigher lines may hide pixels of lower lines at crossings.\n\nRule\nRecover each full line from its visible collinear pixels. At any cell where\nmultiple recovered lines cross, the visible color is above every other line\nthrough that cell. Return the unique ordering of all line colors that respects\nthose above/below constraints and whose consecutive colors are lines that\ngeometrically cross.\n\nKey insight\nThe visible crossing colors give a partial layer order, and the intended answer\nis the unique Hamiltonian chain through the line-intersection graph from the\nbottom layer to the top layer.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2c181942\" lines=\"7-21\">\n<description>\nAttach monochrome sprites to the matching sides of a central hollow square.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. A hollow NxN square (hub) with bg corners/interior and four differently-colored edges (top, bottom, left, right).\n- 2-4 monochrome sprites scattered on the grid, each matching one hub edge color.\n\nRule:\n- For each hub edge color, find the matching sprite outside the hub.\n- Rotate the sprite (0/90/180/270°) so its contact edge has exactly N-2 contiguous cells aligned with the hub side, and the rotated sprite is mirror-symmetric along the contact-edge axis (UD for left/right, LR for top/bottom).\n- Translate it to adjoin that hub side. \n- Clear everything else to bg; output is hub + attached sprites only.\n\nKey insight: Each edge color uniquely pairs a sprite to a side; symmetry along the contact-edge axis disambiguates the correct rotation.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"bd5af378\" lines=\"25-42\">\n<description>\nRewrite a corner L block into two nested L borders and a diagonal trace.\n\nInput structure\nA rectangular grid has exactly two colors: one color forms a one-cell-thick\nL on two adjacent outer edges, and the other color fills the remaining\nrectangle.\n\nRule\nNormalize the L to the top-left corner. Paint the outer top-left L with the\nfill color, the next inner L with the original border color, and the main\ndiagonal of the remaining inner rectangle with the fill color. All other\ncells become color 8, then the original orientation is restored.\n\nKey insight\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
